er.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313389} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d80ee5-4b61-5649-991b-08f557a8dc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313493} {:answer/id #uuid "ea34fcb0-83d8-5c60-9aab-be6de2adf3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313839} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ab1a37-3aeb-50e5-ac0c-f636df7e8865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6adb5300-558b-5171-ae85-ccd08169caac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312814} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3676550-2291-58a0-8716-0b0471438972" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21099cf0-6ade-5176-97e2-98b51987b126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312226} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9b48c8-5e3a-5bc2-a5ab-b517fd3e8a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312487} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73d4a2-4ad0-5837-b408-af5ab8f8dfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312737} {:answer/id #uuid "b835c93d-0f3b-59f6-8a79-699819da02b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313406} {:answer/id #uuid "f35dc895-fc13-50f6-aa56-710cb0aafcfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f73d4a2-4ad0-5837-b408-af5ab8f8dfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312737} :answer-field/id #uuid "f825e814-46db-5a49-9ae3-db2a2ad9a646" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc60bc57-5b9c-5f88-9bc5-5ecffd1981e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340662" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067f1f01-946a-508e-8cd3-212922a30cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312018} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9ff7e5-36fe-5003-9799-45faaef030fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312189} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6042f8-3d6d-5360-9b00-ffce5586d196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312954} {:answer/id #uuid "9d043cac-3ffa-5f03-90e2-e0c96f5392e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313218} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d8c3cb-4ca0-5236-8d59-7a3088019974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d043cac-3ffa-5f03-90e2-e0c96f5392e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313218} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c8bc7b-4cd5-5227-9be1-4358ff24b62e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ffdb817-1342-5108-9142-29c41b65bfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312744} {:answer/id #uuid "65761c3a-2090-56c5-acc5-a7f5ae659917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312774} {:answer/id #uuid "821891c1-b858-5bda-9626-34240d7900c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313023} {:answer/id #uuid "832cde4d-8aee-5316-8503-fd0c2df797ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313031} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ae89cc-5e23-5a8d-aa64-bd8dbd7a3313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ae89cc-5e23-5a8d-aa64-bd8dbd7a3313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313912} :answer-field/id #uuid "82f86a36-0a17-52e0-b0d0-d33c3e50f91e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec31f7d-80af-523d-b38f-294b8ad028bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312081} {:answer/id #uuid "0f14e142-4150-51d0-8ede-ebf4f1bd28f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312085} {:answer/id #uuid "114b3cdc-ae09-538f-94d9-f5c3e4392e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312098} {:answer/id #uuid "992303bc-e624-5324-b23d-d78df9b645e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313189} {:answer/id #uuid "dd23267e-19c8-5ea7-b897-d3089e3d52ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "114b3cdc-ae09-538f-94d9-f5c3e4392e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312098} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b3226d-31b8-5280-8683-73d653641be2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5448f9e-c9df-59b5-abdc-33e558c56456" :question/math-academy-id "q-341309" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198d726b-81cf-594b-83de-76c02bac0919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317648} {:answer/id #uuid "2abe5e98-e65c-5dce-9aca-0d78ba5afc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317698} {:answer/id #uuid "6285297c-4cc8-56cf-a700-fc37fe41f125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317899} {:answer/id #uuid "631bd091-0c21-5342-bc1c-e25588a3d301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317902} {:answer/id #uuid "f95de9e6-1a91-563b-9991-d16ffd905e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f95de9e6-1a91-563b-9991-d16ffd905e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318393} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19d5429-ff07-562a-88c1-4561fba05cb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca2aa98f-e046-5712-ac1b-d633a0352fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-345179" :question/problem "$$\n8x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2{y}^{′} & =-2\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ y & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\ny={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -2{y}^{′} & =-2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} \\\\ &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form ${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$ for some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ 2(4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67eb3bc2-2dae-5bcc-81f3-abdbe6168756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317921} {:answer/id #uuid "7e43e62c-b93f-5769-9d88-912941f4112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317996} {:answer/id #uuid "cba2decb-d76c-5930-8c17-12eac34e5bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318240} {:answer/id #uuid "e25a8cc4-8287-5e43-b435-44b6a425198b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318319} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ae2ac7-1abf-5f30-8776-1acbc51c17cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4ae2ac7-1abf-5f30-8776-1acbc51c17cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318328} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5785ebf-b3d3-57a2-aa7d-442f2512a79b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bcde26e-a814-5a9a-8d44-c07635ebcbd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-345183" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x(x-3){y}^{′}-6y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 3x(x-3){y}^{′} & =(3{x}^{2}-9x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x(x-3){y}^{′}={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(x-3){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -9\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ff28c35-09fb-59ed-a126-96769bf6367b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda -29=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317666} {:answer/id #uuid "58e42ba6-d512-5274-b3f5-240cf898bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317864} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec52643-5110-52fd-a9de-1d9bfff4dd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-28\\lambda +11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317887} {:answer/id #uuid "df288c89-1312-59a5-87dc-6c5f11441eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda -11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318311} {:answer/id #uuid "f7be4292-dc25-5e6f-bb74-ae876a238d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+29\\lambda -11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e42ba6-d512-5274-b3f5-240cf898bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317864} :answer-field/id #uuid "445b9390-8ab7-581e-9f22-a90f1aad99a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a77850c3-4a97-5de2-b594-ea2c1df5ead8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345190" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x(x+7){y}^{′}+11y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -4x(x+7){y}^{′} & =(-4{x}^{2}-28x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 11y & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }11{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-4x(x+7){y}^{′}=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x(x+7){y}^{′} & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[-4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 11y & =11{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }11{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+11{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -28\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= ({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4682987a-1487-5f3b-afb7-6ec5aecce425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317797} {:answer/id #uuid "486c97a8-31cf-50e4-b181-e15e3c368e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317805} {:answer/id #uuid "547bd6ca-243e-5294-8aad-9e15c7783ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317846} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd8b343-618d-5a1b-bd4d-b6b3c12363d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda +3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317984} {:answer/id #uuid "fab7f702-b517-5079-b2a4-aa0c5bc95040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "547bd6ca-243e-5294-8aad-9e15c7783ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317846} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1107813-af0d-5fe8-8af9-ecce3d993831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fb5ceb7-398f-5abf-bba8-56408ac9272d" :question/math-academy-id "q-345272" :question/problem "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+(x+3)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x+3)y & =(x+3){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37163b30-519d-55aa-a935-afaa2a8b6626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317742} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4b0fc3-c005-544c-b7d8-233ac5777e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda -4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317768} {:answer/id #uuid "afe29659-1c77-5d4f-996a-40fe05b00566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318160} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7e1aa2-d04d-562c-8833-a7782e1f0261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318351} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c171c0-4ed8-5d41-be96-6b3c4bd295ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37163b30-519d-55aa-a935-afaa2a8b6626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317742} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7b4019-2d59-5290-ac6a-1c83d4deb85e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b745c996-8892-5b1d-bee0-3efd941d82d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345275" :question/problem "$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-11x{y}^{′}-(x-4)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series so that they use the same index of summation.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -11x{y}^{′} & =-11x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }11(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -(x-4)y & =-(x-4){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n-(x-4)y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -11x{y}^{′} & =-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-11(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-11\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (2{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -11\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e6c6b4-11d1-560c-b3a6-f4d7f83e341b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317597} {:answer/id #uuid "83a44dbc-3b07-5504-8d8f-7f9cbb5872ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318010} {:answer/id #uuid "8cac5b1b-9726-54f9-8aa4-e22bb560aa66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318042} {:answer/id #uuid "921ff929-e818-5c9d-b411-8d9a119d2ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318063} {:answer/id #uuid "a0edb4a3-72a6-5828-a086-7a198aad2d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0edb4a3-72a6-5828-a086-7a198aad2d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318116} :answer-field/id #uuid "4db2629f-e16d-5899-805b-cb60178dd11e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f15206c-389e-5969-948f-ad93b00e844b" :question/math-academy-id "q-345279" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+12x{y}^{′}+(9x-2)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (9{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +12\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6fdd49-ceaf-50e8-b74b-f973a944b0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317616} {:answer/id #uuid "38eb426e-4501-5393-8abf-aedd3b2ef704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317748} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9be647-f45d-55c3-9a85-607dcef9998c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317757} {:answer/id #uuid "c44b8187-dccd-5b12-aac6-e67a8c639b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318215} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ed541e-4c43-5a03-a09d-713bdb1874a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-3/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b9be647-f45d-55c3-9a85-607dcef9998c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317757} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf1aa8c-f7ee-5fb4-ba18-2f23bcccb918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53354b57-f813-51e1-a379-13c900073c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-345281" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n8{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+6x{y}^{′}+(8x-3)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 6x{y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (8x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(8{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 6x{y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (8x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(8{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -3)(2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}.$"}]
